#!/bin/sh
# THE LAST THING BETWEEN THIS CHECKOUT AND main, AND THE ONLY ONE. Wired by the root `prepare` script
# (core.hooksPath=.githooks) alongside commit-msg. It runs _tools/scripts/verify/verify-push.mjs with git's ref lines on
# stdin, so the script knows exactly which range is leaving.
#
# Which is why this repository declares no `push` check in .intentic/checks.json and the sandbox holds no
# "Check before you push" rule: the app pushes by running `git push` in this checkout (no --no-verify), so both of those
# would run this very script a second time, in the same push, minutes apart and against the same tree — and the earlier
# of the two has to guess its range from `@{u}` where this one is told. A refusal here reaches the app as a refusal:
# push-run.ts reads git's exit and attributes it to the hook.
#
# What that script does, cheapest first, stopping at the first refusal: every check the manifest lists
# (_tools/checks/run.mjs: the lockfile, the test programs, the workflow policy, the byte scan, the daemon's
# structural gates and the rest, under two seconds and needing nothing installed), the assertion ratchet over
# the range's test files, the manifest/lockfile lockstep, and the linter; rustfmt on any Rust crate the push
# touches. The three steps CI's verify groups run (typecheck, build, test) are replayed from the verdict the
# land's `pnpm verify` or an earlier push check recorded for this tree, and otherwise left to CI; `pnpm verify:push
# --suite` runs them here. The header of that script says why each tier is there.
#
# No dependency guard: this needs node, which is the thing running the repo; the heavy tier needs pnpm and says so
# when it is missing. `git push --no-verify` when you genuinely mean to push a tree that does not pass.
exec node _tools/scripts/verify/verify-push.mjs --hook
